The persona is real, the top bar says which one, and it stops sounding like a Mac
===================================================================================

QuixiDesk had no notion of a persona: the system prompt was one hardcoded string and the top right of
the window showed a button labelled with a provider COUNT, which tells you nothing about who is
answering. It also carried macOS habits into a Linux build.

The persona is a file the user owns — <data_dir>/persona.md (~/.quixi/persona.md). It is read at the
start of every turn rather than cached at boot, so an edit lands on the next message and an external
editor works too. Its first "# " heading is its name.

  * the engine reads it and prepends it to the system prompt, capped at 24000 chars, framed so the
    persona governs voice while the tool rules still govern how the agent acts here
  * getPersona / setPersona over the existing IPC bridge, reporting name, path, size and loaded state
  * the top bar shows the loaded persona's name instead of a provider count, and opens a page with
    the status, the file path, an editor and Save
  * nothing about the persona lives in localStorage; it is a file on the machine

This edition also says what it is, and how it talks, instead of wearing upstream's voice:

    You are Volkdude's QuixiDesk. You are a tool.

followed by house style — no preamble, no "Great question", swearing allowed, blunt allowed, match the
register, do not hedge what you can know, your own mistakes get one line and a fix. That is style, not
history: no personal facts belong in it.

macOS habits removed from the Linux build:

  * the prompt told the agent to run `open -a` and `osascript` as examples of shell commands; it now
    says `xdg-open` and `systemctl`
  * three help strings rendered a Command-symbol shortcut (the glyph itself, "/Ctrl+N") — a Mac
    keystroke in an Arch application. They now read Ctrl+N, Ctrl+K, Ctrl+,.

Persona files are personal. This patch ships the mechanism and the house style, never a biography:
write your own into ~/.quixi/persona.md or paste it into the editor.

